<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pervez Musharraf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pervez Musharraf was a Pakistani army general who seized power in a coup in October 1999 and ruled until 2008, as chief executive and from 2001 as president. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the attacks of 11 September 2001 and survived several assassination attempts. Faced with impeachment, he resigned in 2008 and later lived in exile in Dubai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf.jpg" alt="Pervez Musharraf" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antônio Cruz/ABr · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pervez_Musharraf_2004_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pervez Musharraf was born on 11 August 1943 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, to an Urdu-speaking family of Sayyids. His father, Syed Musharrafuddin, worked in the foreign office of the British Indian government and became an accounting director, and his mother, Zarin, took a degree in English literature at Delhi University. In 1961, aged 18, he entered the Pakistan Military Academy at Kakul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Musharraf first saw battle with an artillery regiment during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965">Second Kashmir War</a> of 1965 and received the Imtiazi Sanad medal for gallantry. In 1987 he commanded a new brigade of the Special Services Group near the Siachen Glacier. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> promoted him to four-star general and Chief of the Army Staff in October 1998. From March to May 1999 he ordered the secret infiltration of forces into the Kargil district, and a fierce Indian offensive nearly led to a full-scale war. Sharif withdrew support in July under international pressure, which antagonised the army, and Sharif's attempt to dismiss Musharraf was followed by the coup of October 1999.</p><p>Musharraf governed as Chief Executive, appointed himself president on 20 June 2001, and in August 2002 issued the Legal Framework Order, which made many amendments to the Constitution. After the attacks of 11 September 2001 he allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> against the Taliban. His government liberalised the media and presided over rapid economic growth, while his rule also saw curbs on the judiciary and a growing insurgency in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">North-West Frontier Province</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His dismissal of Chief Justice Iftikhar Chaudhry in March 2007 and the state of emergency of November cost him support, and he announced his resignation on 18 August 2008. He founded the All Pakistan Muslim League in 2010. On 17 December 2019 a special court sentenced him in absentia to death for suspending the constitution in November 2007. He died on 5 February 2023, aged 79, of amyloidosis, and was buried with military honours in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. Judgements of his rule are sharply divided between those who credit him with economic stabilisation and those who point to the erosion of democratic institutions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pervez Musharraf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in American Hospital Dubai, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">the United Arab Emirates</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1995" aria-label="Delhi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6568 1115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pervez Musharraf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7018 1385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1995">What was here in 1995? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
